            <ArticleTitle>Pathophysiology of resistant hypertension in chronic kidney disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>571-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.semnephrol.2014.08.011</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0270-9295(14)00116-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hypertension associated with chronic kidney diseases often is resistant to drug treatment. This review deals with two main aspects of the management of CKD patients with hypertension: the role of sodium/volume and the need for dietary salt restriction, as well as appropriate use of diuretics and what currently is called sequential nephron blockade; the second aspect that is addressed extensively in this review is the role of the sympathetic nervous system and the possible clinical use of renal denervation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
